From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 02:50:22 +0200
Subject: media: sun6i-isp: Add the rotator as a mode of a capture channel

The ISP has a rotator, but it is not an output of its own: it reads back
what one of the two channels wrote and lays a turned copy through its own
addresses, and which channel it reads is a bit in its config register. So
it belongs to a channel as a mode rather than as a third video device, and
it is exposed as V4L2_CID_ROTATE on both capture nodes.

The buffer carries both images. The channel writes its own untuned output
into a scratch region past the end of the rotated one, page aligned, and
the rotator lays the turned copy at offset zero - which is where userspace
expects the frame, and what the payload length covers. That is the
vendor's arrangement, where one buffer holds every output at its own
offset with the one that matters at zero.

Only a quarter turn uses it. A half turn is a mirror and a flip at once,
which the channel does for free and without a second image in the buffer,
and it is also the only way to get 180 out of this hardware: the rotator
produces nothing at all at that angle. Being a mirror and a flip, it
composes with those two controls rather than overriding them, so 180 plus
a vertical flip leaves a plain mirror.

There is one rotator, so the second channel to ask for a quarter turn is
refused rather than quietly taking it from the first - which would leave
that channel's buffers laid out for a rotation that is no longer
happening. The enable is programmed from the configuration pass rather
than by whichever channel wants it, because the load buffer keeps its
contents across streams and a run that left the bit set would have the
rotator working against a freed buffer.

The enable lives in MODULE_EN alongside the tuning modules, so the params
side has to leave it alone: a params buffer arriving mid-stream must not
turn off a rotator the capture side is using.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
